145: {skill_tested: "Identify an initial belief.", question: "Why does Nia first suspect Mr. Vale?", option1: "He admits closing the main valve.", option2: "He refuses to enter the utility room.", option3: "His prior complaints and calm expression seem to suggest he expected the outage.", option4: "He owns the courtyard hose.", correctAnswer: "His prior complaints and calm expression seem to suggest he expected the outage.", explanation: "Step 1: Nia misreads his complaints and calm as satisfaction.<br><br>Answer: His prior complaints and calm expression seem to suggest he expected the outage.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
146: {skill_tested: "Interpret a comparison.", question: "Tracing the pipes feels like tracing an argument because:", option1: "every pipe contains written sentences.", option2: "Nia wants to debate the residents.", option3: "the plumbing follows the newspaper layout.", option4: "important connections are hidden between visible sections.", correctAnswer: "important connections are hidden between visible sections.", explanation: "Step 1: Both systems require reconstructing missing links.<br><br>Answer: important connections are hidden between visible sections.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
147: {skill_tested: "Identify central theme.", question: "Which theme is most central to the passage?", option1: "Careful evidence can correct both practical problems and premature judgments.", option2: "Old buildings should always be replaced.", option3: "Public praise is the best reward for skill.", option4: "Speed matters more than caution during emergencies.", correctAnswer: "Careful evidence can correct both practical problems and premature judgments.", explanation: "Step 1: Nia solves the outage and revises her opinion through evidence.<br><br>Answer: Careful evidence can correct both practical problems and premature judgments.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
148: {skill_tested: "Analyze characterization.", question: "Mr. Vale stops Nia from turning the hidden valve chiefly because he is:", option1: "unwilling to let younger people help.", option2: "methodical about consequences that have not yet been ruled out.", option3: "certain the valve is unrelated.", option4: "afraid residents will hear the pipes.", correctAnswer: "methodical about consequences that have not yet been ruled out.", explanation: "Step 1: He requires evidence before an action that might cause flooding.<br><br>Answer: methodical about consequences that have not yet been ruled out.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
149: {skill_tested: "Trace reasoning.", question: "The third-floor resident's observation helps Nia by:", option1: "proving the roof tank is empty.", option2: "identifying who moved the cabinet.", option3: "locating the interruption relative to where pipe branches divide.", option4: "showing every apartment failed simultaneously.", correctAnswer: "locating the interruption relative to where pipe branches divide.", explanation: "Step 1: Different failure timing narrows the blockage location.<br><br>Answer: locating the interruption relative to where pipe branches divide.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
150: {skill_tested: "Analyze structure.", question: "The traveling cheer after the valve turns primarily:", option1: "introduces a second plumbing failure.", option2: "shows residents did not notice the outage.", option3: "causes Nia to discard the map.", option4: "confirms the solution through a sound that moves across the building.", correctAnswer: "confirms the solution through a sound that moves across the building.", explanation: "Step 1: The sequence makes restored flow audible and spatial.<br><br>Answer: confirms the solution through a sound that moves across the building.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
151: {skill_tested: "Infer motivation.", question: "Why does Nia ask about Mr. Vale's expression instead of immediately apologizing?", option1: "She seeks to understand him without making him manage her guilt.", option2: "She still believes he caused the outage.", option3: "She wants him to praise her publicly.", option4: "She has forgotten her earlier suspicion.", correctAnswer: "She seeks to understand him without making him manage her guilt.", explanation: "Step 1: The narration explicitly rejects an apology centered on comforting Nia.<br><br>Answer: She seeks to understand him without making him manage her guilt.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
152: {skill_tested: "Interpret the final note.", question: "The note Check before turning functions most importantly as:", option1: "proof that no one may enter the room.", option2: "both a safety instruction and a reminder to examine assumptions.", option3: "a criticism of the plumber's delay.", option4: "an order to leave every valve closed.", correctAnswer: "both a safety instruction and a reminder to examine assumptions.", explanation: "Step 1: Its literal and figurative meanings unite the outage with Nia's changed judgment.<br><br>Answer: both a safety instruction and a reminder to examine assumptions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
153: {skill_tested: "Synthesize character development.", question: "Nia's development is best described as a movement from:", option1: "avoiding responsibility to accepting public praise.", option2: "trusting maps to rejecting all plans.", option3: "assigning blame from appearances to testing connections before concluding.", option4: "working independently to refusing Mr. Vale's help.", correctAnswer: "assigning blame from appearances to testing connections before concluding.", explanation: "Step 1: Her investigative method becomes an ethical lesson about judgment.<br><br>Answer: assigning blame from appearances to testing connections before concluding.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Character and Relationship Analysis", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
